<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Saint Basil's Cathedral</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1555">1555</a>–<a href="/years/1561">1561</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Cathedral of Vasily the Blessed</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Saint Basil's Cathedral, formally the Cathedral of the Intercession of the Most Holy Theotokos on the Moat, stands on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-square">Red Square</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> had it built in 1555–1561 to commemorate the capture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a>, and its cluster of chapels crowned with colourful onion domes made it a symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. It takes its popular name from the holy fool Basil, buried in a chapel added in 1588.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/saint-basils-cathedral.jpg" alt="Saint Basil's Cathedral" width="320" height="355" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Tsy1980 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Saint_Basil%27s_Cathedral_in_Moscow.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The site was a busy marketplace between the gate of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a> and the outlying settlement. Its centre was marked by the Trinity Church, built of white stone. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-iv-of-russia">Ivan IV</a> marked each victory of the Russo-Kazan War by building a wooden memorial church beside Trinity Church, and by the end of his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astrakhan">Astrakhan</a> campaign it was surrounded by seven wooden churches. In the autumn of 1554, according to Nikon's Chronicle, he ordered a wooden Church of the Intercession on the moat, and a year later a new stone cathedral on the site of Trinity Church to commemorate his campaigns. Dedicating a church to a military victory was a major innovation in Muscovy.</p><p>The building was consecrated on 12 July 1561 as Trinity Church, after its easternmost sanctuary. Its popular name <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a> arose in the late 16th century, and the name of Vasily the Blessed, who died during construction and was buried on the site, was attached to it at the beginning of the 17th century.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">What it is</h2><p>Ivan's architects chose a symmetrical plan with eight side churches around a central core. The core and the four larger churches on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnetic-compass">compass</a> points are octagonal, and the four smaller churches between them are cuboid. The central Church of the Intercession is 46 metres tall inside but has a floor area of only 64 square metres. The foundations are of white stone and the churches of red brick, then a relatively new material, and restorers in 1954–1955 found that the brick walls conceal a wooden frame, built as a life-size model of the cathedral and gradually enclosed in masonry.</p><p>The building has no analogue in earlier or later Muscovite architecture, and the sources that inspired its builders Barma and Postnik are disputed. Nineteenth-century Russian writers, starting with Ivan Zabelin, stressed the vernacular wooden churches of the Russian North, while a modern hypothesis sees it as a recreation of the Qolşärif Mosque at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kazan">Kazan</a>. A chapel over the grave of Vasily was added in 1588, and in 1680–1683 arcades and galleries joined the nine churches into one monolithic building.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>In the 16th and 17th centuries the church was perceived as the earthly symbol of the Heavenly City, and it served as an allegory of the Jerusalem Temple in the Palm Sunday parade, in which the tsar led the patriarch of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on a donkey. Under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/soviet-union">Soviet Union</a>'s antireligious campaigns it was confiscated from the Russian Orthodox community and has been part of the State Historical Museum since 1928. It became part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow-kremlin">Moscow Kremlin</a> and Red Square UNESCO World Heritage Site in 1990, and weekly liturgical celebrations have been restored since 1997. The Russian historian Dmitry Shvidkovsky wrote that it is like no other Russian building.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Saint Basil's Cathedral” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
